+## #549 — pyright baseline ratchet (cmdr-P, 2026-06-28)
+
+**Mission:** Ratchet 71 pre-existing pyright errors toward zero with type-only fixes across groups A–I.
+
+**Outcome:** 71 → 2 errors (−69, 98%). All 9 in-fence groups fixed. 2 remaining = cmdr-T's fence (session_coast.py, session_traction.py), deferred per launch order fence rule. 675 tests green throughout.
+
+**What worked well:**
+- G1–G4 gate structure matched the problem well. Groups F+G+H batched cleanly in G1; group I misc into G2; B+C into G3; D+E into G4.
+- Implementer subagents handled the mechanical changes reliably. The handoffs with exact change instructions + verification commands were the right format.
+- Reasoning reviews (reasoning gates with crew waiver) were correct for these changes — independent verification via pyright + test runner meant no human reviewer adds signal.
+- `cast(float, x)` vs `float(x)` for pandas Scalar: the G3 implementer found the right fix when the handoff's suggested `float()` was rejected by pyright's `ConvertibleToFloat` check. Good example of implementer deviation improving on the handoff.
+
+**Where the workflow added friction:**
+
+- **g1-integrate postcondition c2**: The engine requires the `review-result` artifact with `verdict=APPROVE` to be attached to `g1-integrate` (not just `g1-review`). The pattern is: attach the review-result to BOTH the `gN-review` gate AND the `gN-integrate` gate. Not stated clearly in the template — improvised each time.
+- **Crew idle-strands-deliverable (G1 implementer)**: G1 implementer made all code changes correctly but did not write the result file. Commander had to ground-truth and write it from verified evidence. This is a recurring pattern — subagents implement then return text summary without writing the artifact. Template instruction "Write your result to: ..." needs to be more prominent or a pre-condition enforced by run_crew.py.
+- **advance g1-integrate before review-result attached**: The first `advance` call for g1-integrate failed with "postconditions unmet ['c2']" because the review-result wasn't attached. The correct sequence is: attach review-result artifact to gN-integrate BEFORE calling advance. Needs to be explicit in the skill instructions.
+- **Engine gates are sequential**: G3 and G4 target disjoint file sets (no conflicts), but the engine gate ordering prevents parallel dispatch. For future type-audit runs with many independent groups, consider whether the execute.json should use a flattened gate structure or whether the engine needs a "concurrent" gate type.
+- **pyright count differs Windows/CI**: Local Windows py3.14 count may differ from CI ubuntu/py3.11 (as warned in launch order). Verified that the runtime baseline-diff gate handles this correctly — the gate compares the same environment baseline vs HEAD in CI. No action needed but worth keeping as a standing note.
+- **origin/main advanced during the run**: cmdr-T's #546 merged to main while this branch was in-flight. `git diff origin/main --name-only` showed those files misleadingly in my diff. Always diff against the merge-base (`git diff 8a19c5bc HEAD`) to see only your changes.
+
+**Lesson candidates (for lessons-delta.json):**
+- `cast-not-float-for-pandas-scalar`: use `cast(float, x)` not `float(x)` when narrowing pandas `itertuples()` row attributes; pyright's `float.__new__` `ConvertibleToFloat` check rejects `Scalar` union.
+- `g-integrate-needs-review-result-artifact`: attach `review-result` artifact to `gN-integrate` (not just `gN-review`) for the c2 engine-checked postcondition to pass.
+- `diff-against-merge-base-not-main`: when origin/main advances during a run, `git diff origin/main` shows upstream changes; use `git diff <merge-base> HEAD` to see only your commits.
+
+**Template Update Candidates:** None — the fixes are procedural (engine usage), not template content gaps.
+
